/*
 * The Elite Dangerous ship catalogue — every player-flyable hull's identity, its
 * per-hull stats, and its outfitting slot layout, in one record per hull.
 *
 * Sources: EDCD FDevIDs (identity), EDCD/coriolis-data (stats and slot layout), EDSY
 * (journal slot names, and the Lynx Highliner coriolis-data does not carry), and
 * in-game verification.
 *
 * Credit and licence terms: ATTRIBUTIONS.md. Acquisition, revisions, derivation and
 * corrections: data/ships/SOURCES.md.
 *
 * `symbol` is the internal identifier (e.g. `Empire_Trader`); `name` the display
 * name; `manufacturer` the shipyard manufacturer; `size` the small/medium/large
 * landing-pad class; `entitlement` the DLC/grant token on gated hulls. Stats (`hullMass`
 * in tonnes, `minimumSpeed`/`maximumSpeed`/`boost` in m/s, rotation rates in deg/s, …)
 * and the slot layout
 * (`core` sizes, `hardpoints`, `utility` count, `optional` mounts) are present for
 * every hull. A `hardpoints` or `optional` mount is `{ size }`, plus a
 * `restriction` when the mount only takes one family of modules — the Type-11
 * Prospector's four mining hardpoints and its limpet-controller and vessel-hangar
 * optionals, the Panther Clipper Mk II's two cargo-only optionals, the Lynx
 * Highliner's three passenger-cabin-only optionals, the military mounts 16 hulls
 * have, and the planetary-approach-suite mount all 48 hulls have.
 *
 * A mount also carries a `name` where the game's journal slot key is not what the
 * ordinary numbering rules produce — 13 hulls, being the 10 whose names no rule
 * derives plus 3 the rules do derive, pinned so the stored set matches its source one
 * for one. Among the 10: the Anaconda's `Slot13_Size2`/`Slot14_Size1` with no 11 or
 * 12, the Type-9 Heavy starting at `Slot00_Size8`, the Type-8 Transporter skipping
 * `SmallHardpoint3`, and the Caspian Explorer's mediums running 6, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4; the
 * other 3 are the Panther Clipper Mk II, the Type-11 Prospector and the Lynx
 * Highliner, whose `Passenger01`–`03` the rules reach because a restricted mount
 * consumes no `SlotNN` number. SOURCES.md lists all 13, hull by hull. A mount
 * with no `name` is one the rules get right, and a hull that names any mount of a kind
 * names all of them. A key's `_SizeN` suffix is Frontier's own and on three hulls
 * disagrees with the mount it names (the Keelback's `Slot03_Size3` is a size-4
 * mount) — the mount's own `size` beside it is always the real one.
 *
 * A hull's armour options are modules like any other: they live in
 * `modules-core.jsonc`, keyed to the hull by their `ship` field. Most come from coriolis-data; the Lynx Highliner
 * (`MediumTransport01`) instead uses EDSY and Frontier update-note values documented
 * in `SOURCES.md`. `hullCost` is the bare hull's standard price in credits and
 * `retailCost` the hull with its default modules; both are present for every hull.
 * The modules behind that retail fit live separately in `default-loadouts.jsonc`, so a
 * consumer importing hull identity and geometry does not bundle fitted-module rows.
 */
